//! {{project-name}}: gameplay shared between `cargo run` and the
//! editor's Play button.
//!
//! Scene content (entities, components, positions) lives in
//! `assets/*.bsn` files authored in the editor. Game behaviour
//! (systems, observers, resources) lives in [`GamePlugin`].
//!
//! # Try it
//!
//! 1. Open this project in jackdaw.
//! 2. Outliner: click `Add Entity` and pick `Cube`.
//! 3. Components panel: click `Add Component` and pick `SpinningCube`.
//!    Set `speed` to `1.5`.
//! 4. `File > Save`, then click Play. The cube spins.
//! 5. `cargo run` launches the same game without the editor.
//!
//! # Adding your own components
//!
//! Write a component anywhere in this library (any module, not
//! `main.rs`), deriving `Component, Reflect, Default` with
//! `#[reflect(Component, Default)]`, like `SpinningCube` below. After you
//! save, click Rebuild in jackdaw (or run `jd build`) and it
//! appears in `Add Component`. No registration code is needed.
//!
//! # Tuning parameters (game-wide, saved as an asset)
//!
//! `GameConfig` below is the other half of the pattern: a *Resource*
//! whole systems read (`falling_cubes` uses `gravity` and
//! `move_speed`) whose value comes from an asset file,
//! `assets/game-config.bsn`. To change the tuning:
//!
//! 1. In jackdaw, `Project window > New Asset...` and pick
//!    `GameConfig` (after one Rebuild) — the editor creates
//!    `game-config.bsn` and opens its card.
//! 2. Edit `move_speed` (say `2.5`), `Save`.
//! 3. Play: the falling cube falls and spins by the file's numbers.
//!    `cargo run` and an exported build read the same file.
//!
//! Until that file exists the game runs on the `Default` values — a
//! missing config is not an error. Per-entity numbers (one cube's
//! `speed`) stay as components; game-wide numbers (gravity, move
//! speed, player health) belong in a config like this.
//!
//! # Input actions
//!
//! The same pattern drives input: `InputActionsPlugin` evaluates the
//! built-in actions (Move / Look / Jump / Interact / Pause — WASD and
//! the left stick, mouse and the right stick, Space / South, E / East,
//! Escape / Start) into the `GameActions` resource every frame; systems
//! read named actions instead of polling raw keys. To rebind, author
//! `assets/input-actions.bsn` the same way as the tuning config (the
//! plugin below wires the file; without it, the built-in table runs).
//!
//! In the editor's Play, click the game image to capture input and
//! Shift+Esc to give it back — a plain Esc is forwarded to the game
//! (that is why Pause can be Escape).
//!
//! # Physics & triggers
//!
//! The physics module (on by default) adds avian: things fall, land
//! and stay on colliders. Three pieces worth knowing:
//!
//! * **Colliders**: add an `AvianCollider` component to any entity
//!   (the editor's physics category) and pick a shape. A collider
//!   with no rigid body stands still as a wall or floor; a
//!   `RigidBody: Dynamic` one falls and lands — never through a
//!   floor. The falling cube and the pickups below are the examples.
//! * **Triggers**: an entity that is a collider *and* carries
//!   `Sensor` detects overlaps without blocking anything (the editor
//!   draws sensors with a cyan outline). Each frame,
//!   `TriggerEvents` lists every sensor that started or stopped
//!   touching something: `enters()` for pickup-style "got it",
//!   `exits()` for "left the area". The plugin wires the
//!   `CollisionEventsEnabled` prerequisite avian asks for — author
//!   the `Sensor`, nothing else. `collect_pickups` below is the
//!   whole pattern: the player walks into a pickup, it despawns and
//!   the count goes up — only the player's touch counts, so a cube
//!   settling onto a pickup leaves it alone; collect all three and
//!   the level clears.
//! * **Collision layers**: a `CollisionLayers` component (fields
//!   `memberships` and `filters`, one bit per layer) decides who
//!   interacts with whom — a contact only exists when each side's
//!   memberships meet a layer the other side filters in. A common
//!   table: layer 1 the player and the world, layer 2 pickups,
//!   layer 3 hazards. Give hazards `memberships 0b100,
//!   filters 0b001` (hazard hits player only) and the player
//!   `0b001, 0b111` (hits everything) — pickups and hazards never
//!   interact with each other, so a drifting hazard cannot collect
//!   the pickups for the player. `spawn_hazards` below authors
//!   exactly that hazard row; the template player needs nothing
//!   (an entity without the component is on layer 1 with every
//!   filter, which is the table's player row already).
//!
//! The triggers feed more than pickups: `collect_pickups` counts and
//! `hazard_damage` burns health — same shape, different marker and
//! different reaction, and both read the one `TriggerEvents` the
//! bridge fills.
//!
//! Two gravities exist and they are different things:
//! `GameConfig.gravity` (the config asset above) is the author's
//! number, and at startup `apply_gravity_from_config` copies it
//! into the physics engine's `Gravity` resource — the one the
//! simulation actually uses. Editing the config file changes the
//! next launch; changing the `Gravity` resource in the editor's
//! Resources panel changes it for the running session.
//!
//! # Player controller
//!
//! The player the template spawns at (-2, 1, 0) is a real, if
//! minimal, character controller:
//!
//! * **Tuning is a component.** `PlayerController { move_speed,
//!   jump_speed }` lives on the player entity: select it and edit
//!   the numbers in the Components panel (per-entity numbers are
//!   components; game-wide numbers are the config above — the same
//!   split as one cube's `speed` vs the config's `move_speed`).
//! * **Movement is camera-relative.** W walks away from the camera
//!   wherever it looks; the body turns smoothly to face the way it
//!   moves. The one source of truth for "which way is forward" is
//!   the camera's `CameraLook.yaw`.
//! * **The camera is a child.** A `CameraRig` parented under the
//!   player is the whole follow-camera setup: `JackdawCameraRigPlugin`
//!   (wired in `GamePlugin` below) activates a lone rig by itself and
//!   drives it around its parent every frame. Author a second rig
//!   only together with an `ActiveCameraRig` component — with two
//!   rigs none self-activates; the game must choose.
//! * **Look has two channels that agree.** The `Look` action writes
//!   the rig's `CameraLook` every frame (`look_into_camera` below);
//!   while the right mouse button is held the rig's own mouse-look
//!   owns the turn instead — the bridge yields those frames so the
//!   two never double-write.
//! * **Jump and collision come from the physics module** (on by
//!   default): gravity — the very same `GameConfig.gravity` number
//!   the engine got — integrates onto the player's velocity, a short
//!   downward cast says when the feet are on the ground, and avian's
//!   `move_and_slide` sweeps the capsule so the player slides along
//!   walls instead of through them (walk into the stage walls to see
//!   it: head-on you stop, diagonally you slide). Without the physics
//!   module the player still walks, camera-relative and
//!   frame-rate-independent, but does not jump or collide.
{{module_physics_player_doc}}
//!
//! # Game UI
//!
//! `assets/hud.bsn` is a UI scene: the same kind of document as
//! `scene.bsn`, authored on the editor's 2D canvas (`Add` menu, `UI`
//! group — panels, labels, buttons, progress bars) with each widget's
//! `Bindings` component wiring it to the game. The template spawns it
//! as a second scene root (`spawn_hud` below), and the four screens
//! inside — start, HUD, pause, game over — show and hide themselves:
//!
//! * **Screens follow state.** Each screen's `Visibility` is bound to
//!   a `GameState` bool (`menu` / `paused` / `game_over`, the
//!   resource below). Flipping the bool is the whole mechanism; the
//!   hud's own screen is bound through the registered `not`, so it is
//!   up whenever the menu is not.
//! * **Buttons say which one was pressed.** Every button carries an
//!   `Action` binding that sends `ScreenPressed` with `button` set to
//!   `"start"`, `"resume"` or `"restart"`; the
//!   `on_screen_pressed` observer turns those into real behavior —
//!   leave the menu, unpause, put the run back the way it began.
//!   The Pause *action* (Escape) lands on the same bool through
//!   `handle_pause_action`, so the key and the button agree.
//! * **The bar and the texts read the player.** The health bar's
//!   `Progress.value` is bound to `PlayerHealth.current` and `.max`
//!   through the registered `ratio` function, and the texts are
//!   `Text` bindings on the same components. Bindings can only call
//!   functions the game registers — that is what the
//!   `register_function` lines below are for. `wire_hud` is the
//!   other half: it puts a `BindContext(player)` on the document's
//!   `HudRoot` once it spawns, which is what `PlayerHealth...`
//!   resolves against.
//!
//! Edit `hud.bsn` in the editor, `Save`, and the running game picks
//! it up like `scene.bsn`. Layout discipline for your own screens:
//! percent and flex for anything position-like (the overlays stretch
//! to 100% of the window, the HUD hugs the top-left corner), pixels
//! only for sizes and padding — that is what keeps the four screens
//! correct from 800x600 up to 2560x1440. One editor edge to know:
//! Play with a UI scene tab focused swaps the 3D scene for that UI
//! alone (it previews the canvas); play from the 3D scene tab to run
//! the game.


use bevy::prelude::*;
use jackdaw_runtime::prelude::*;

/// Your game's Bevy plugin. `main.rs` adds this for both `cargo run`
/// and the editor's Play button (same binary).
#[derive(Default)]
pub struct GamePlugin;

impl Plugin for GamePlugin {
    fn build(&self, app: &mut App) {
        // `spawn_hud` after `spawn_scene`, chained: the editor's Play
        // swaps whichever scene root landed at the LOWEST entity index,
        // and an unordered tuple is a coin flip on which root that is —
        // a swapped hud root means Play runs with no UI at all (T12-T2
        // caught this live). Chaining pins the main scene as first.
        app.add_systems(Startup, (spawn_scene, spawn_hud).chain())
            .add_systems(Update, spin_cubes)
            // One line per config type: register it as an asset (so the
            // editor offers it in New Asset) and load `game-config.bsn`
            // as the starting value of the `GameConfig` resource.
            .init_asset::<GameConfig>()
            .register_asset_reflect::<GameConfig>()
            .add_plugins(ConfigPlugin::<GameConfig>::default())
            .add_systems(Startup, spawn_falling_cube)
            .add_systems(Update, falling_cubes)
            // Input: the built-in five actions evaluate every frame; the
            // `input-actions.bsn` asset (same New Asset flow) overrides
            // bindings and deadzones when the game registers the type.
            .init_asset::<GameInputConfig>()
            .register_asset_reflect::<GameInputConfig>()
            .add_plugins(InputActionsPlugin)
            .add_plugins(ConfigPlugin::<GameInputConfig>::new(
                "input-actions.bsn",
            ))
            .add_systems(Startup, spawn_player)
            .add_systems(Update, (move_player, look_into_camera))
            // The camera rig driver: activates the rig under the player
            // and follows it every frame (see "Player controller" above).
            .add_plugins(JackdawCameraRigPlugin)
            // Game UI (see "Game UI" above): the state the four screens
            // bind to, the functions their bindings call, the observer
            // their buttons feed, and the systems that move the state.
            // Types (`GameState`, `PlayerHealth`, `ScreenPressed`) need
            // no registration — the `Reflect` derives announce them the
            // same way they reach the editor's Add Component list; the
            // via functions are the one binding piece a game registers
            // by hand.
            .init_resource::<GameState>()
            .register_function(ratio)
            .register_function(is_zero)
            .register_function(not)
            .add_observer(on_screen_pressed)
            .add_systems(
                Update,
                // Chained: the player spawns with a zeroed `PlayerHealth`,
                // and an `update_game_over` that ran first would read
                // current <= 0 on frame one and boot the game straight
                // into game over (T12-T2 caught this live in PIE).
                (wire_hud, (apply_health_from_config, update_game_over).chain()),
            )
            // The pause flip owns the frame: a Pause press never also
            // moves the player, whichever way the bool lands. Registered
            // HERE and only here — adding it twice (tuple + edge) runs
            // the toggle twice per press and Escape never pauses
            // (T12-T2 caught this live).
            .add_systems(Update, handle_pause_action.before(move_player));
{{module_physics_statements}}
    }
}

/// Load the authored scene; `JackdawPlugin` spawns its entities as
/// children of this root. The project's `assets/game.flow.json` names
/// the scene a standalone run starts from (`start_scene`); launched by
/// the editor's Play, the game plays the scene the editor has in front
/// (any scene, saved or not) — the override composes around the flow
/// read. A broken or missing flow file warns and falls back to
/// `scene.bsn` here.
fn spawn_scene(mut commands: Commands, asset_server: Res<AssetServer>) {
    commands.spawn(JackdawSceneRoot(
        asset_server.load(jackdaw_runtime::pie_scene_or(
            &jackdaw_runtime::flow::start_scene_or("scene.bsn"),
        )),
    ));
}

/// Spin-rate in radians per second. Attach it in the Components panel; the
/// editor sees this component with no registration code because Bevy's
/// `reflect_auto_register` picks up the `Reflect` derive. `Default` +
/// `#[reflect(Default)]` let the editor add it and the game reconstruct
/// it with sensible starting values.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct SpinningCube {
    pub speed: f32,
}

fn spin_cubes(time: Res<Time>, mut cubes: Query<(&SpinningCube, &mut Transform)>) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs();
    for (cube, mut transform) in &mut cubes {
        transform.rotate_y(cube.speed * dt);
    }
}

/// Game-wide tuning parameters. Both an `Asset` (the editor authors
/// `game-config.bsn` files of it) and a `Resource` (systems read it),
/// so the value can be edited in the editor, saved to disk, and loaded
/// back as the starting value in every run — see "Tuning parameters"
/// above. `player_health` is the run's starting health:
/// `apply_health_from_config` hands it to the player's `PlayerHealth`
/// component when the file loads — the config is the author's intent,
/// the component is the live state after that.
#[derive(Asset, Resource, Reflect, Clone)]
#[reflect(Resource, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Game Config"))]
pub struct GameConfig {
    /// The health a run starts at, handed to the player's
    /// `PlayerHealth` (see "Game UI" above).
    pub player_health: f32,
    /// Multiplies how fast things move and spin.
    pub move_speed: f32,
    /// Downward acceleration, in units per second squared.
    pub gravity: f32,
}

impl Default for GameConfig {
    /// Sensible numbers a game runs on before anyone has authored
    /// `game-config.bsn` — a missing config is not an error.
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            player_health: 100.0,
            move_speed: 1.0,
            gravity: -9.81,
        }
    }
}

/// A cube the config drives, spawned so there is something to watch:
/// it falls under gravity and spins at its component's `speed`
/// scaled by `GameConfig::move_speed` — the per-entity number and
/// the game-wide number working together.
///
/// With the physics module (the default) the fall is the engine's:
/// the cube is a dynamic body with its own collider, dropped onto
/// the stage `spawn_stage` lays below it, so it lands and stays.
/// The spin stays the game's own — a system, not a force.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct FallingCube {
    pub speed: f32,
}

fn spawn_falling_cube(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    // No `SpinningCube` here: two systems rotating one entity stack
    // their rates, which reads as the config having no effect. The
    // falling cube's spin belongs to `move_speed` alone, so one value
    // changes one behavior.
    commands.spawn((
        FallingCube { speed: 1.0 },
        Mesh3d(meshes.add(Cuboid::new(0.6, 0.6, 0.6))),
        MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
            base_color: Color::srgb(0.9, 0.9, 0.9),
            ..default()
        })),
        Transform::from_xyz(0.0, 3.0, 0.0),
{{module_physics_falling_body}}
    ));
}

fn falling_cubes(
    time: Res<Time>,
    config: Res<GameConfig>,
    mut cubes: Query<(&FallingCube, &mut Transform)>,
) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs();
    for (cube, mut transform) in &mut cubes {
{{module_physics_manual_gravity}}
        transform.rotate_y(cube.speed * config.move_speed * dt);
    }
}

/// Marks the entity the five input actions drive. The template spawns
/// one complete; the full set a hand-authored player needs is listed
/// under "Player controller" above (with physics: five components,
/// two without). Its camera is a rig parented under it — or an
/// `ActiveCameraRig` when another rig already exists.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct PlayerMarker;

/// The author's half of the player: the tuning the Components panel
/// edits and the scene saves. Per-entity numbers belong on the entity,
/// game-wide numbers in `GameConfig` — the same split as `SpinningCube`'s
/// `speed` against the config's `move_speed`.
#[derive(Component, Reflect)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct PlayerController {
    /// Ground speed, metres per second.
    pub move_speed: f32,
    /// Launch speed of a jump, metres per second (straight up).
    pub jump_speed: f32,
}

impl Default for PlayerController {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            move_speed: 4.0,
            jump_speed: 4.5,
        }
    }
}

fn spawn_player(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    // The visible body. The capsule matches the collider below, so what
    // you see is what collides. `PlayerHealth` is what the hud reads:
    // it starts at the `Default` and `apply_health_from_config` (below)
    // hands the config's `player_health` over as soon as it loads.
    let player = commands
        .spawn((
            PlayerMarker,
            PlayerController::default(),
            PlayerHealth::default(),
            Mesh3d(meshes.add(Capsule3d::new(0.35, 1.0))),
            MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
                base_color: Color::srgb(0.25, 0.55, 0.9),
                ..default()
            })),
            Transform::from_translation(PLAYER_SPAWN),
{{module_physics_player_body}}
        ))
        .id();
    // The follow camera: a rig parented to the player. Parenting is the
    // whole setup — the plugin above activates a lone rig by itself and
    // drives it around its parent every frame.
    commands.spawn((CameraRig::default(), ChildOf(player)));
}

/// The horizontal direction the Move action asks for this frame, in
/// world space but relative to the camera: rotate the stick/WASD input
/// by the camera's yaw so W is always "away from the camera", then
/// clamp to unit length so diagonals are not faster.
fn move_direction(move_input: Vec2, camera_yaw: f32) -> Vec3 {
    let forward = Quat::from_rotation_y(camera_yaw) * -Vec3::Z;
    let right = Quat::from_rotation_y(camera_yaw) * Vec3::X;
    let mut direction = right * move_input.x + forward * move_input.y;
    direction.y = 0.0;
    if direction.length_squared() > 1.0 {
        direction = direction.normalize();
    }
    direction
}

/// How quickly the body turns to face where it walks: the fraction of
/// the turn to take scales with this, so the turn takes roughly a
/// tenth of a second at any frame rate.
const PLAYER_TURN_RATE: f32 = 12.0;

/// Turn `transform` to face `direction` smoothly — the body runs to
/// face where it walks instead of snapping. Skipped when there is no
/// movement this frame.
fn face_movement_direction(direction: Vec3, dt: f32, transform: &mut Transform) {
    let horizontal = Vec3::new(direction.x, 0.0, direction.z);
    if horizontal.length_squared() < 1e-6 {
        return;
    }
    let facing = horizontal.normalize();
    // `-Z` faces the rotation's "forward"; solve for the yaw that puts
    // it along `facing`.
    let target = Quat::from_rotation_y((-facing.x).atan2(-facing.z));
    let turn = (PLAYER_TURN_RATE * dt).clamp(0.0, 1.0);
    transform.rotation = transform.rotation.slerp(target, turn);
}

/// The Look action drives the rig's orientation: mouse (and right
/// stick) motion becomes `CameraLook` yaw and pitch — the same state
/// the rig's own right-mouse-drag channel writes. While that button is
/// held the bridge yields, so the two channels never double-write the
/// same turn.
fn look_into_camera(
    actions: Res<GameActions>,
    mouse: Option<Res<ButtonInput<MouseButton>>>,
    mut rigs: Query<(&CameraRig, &mut CameraLook)>,
) {
    if mouse
        .as_deref()
        .is_some_and(|buttons| buttons.pressed(MouseButton::Right))
    {
        return;
    }
    let look = actions.value("Look");
    if look == Vec2::ZERO {
        return;
    }
    for (rig, mut camera) in &mut rigs {
        camera.yaw -= look.x * rig.sensitivity;
        camera.pitch = match rig.mode {
            // Third person: look down increases the pitch, bounded to
            // stay above the horizon.
            CameraMode::ThirdPerson => {
                (camera.pitch + look.y * rig.sensitivity).clamp(0.05, 1.4)
            }
            // First person: look up increases the pitch, bounded to
            // stay short of straight up/down.
            CameraMode::FirstPerson => {
                (camera.pitch - look.y * rig.sensitivity).clamp(-1.4, 1.4)
            }
        };
    }
}

// ---- Game UI: the four screens in assets/hud.bsn --------------------
// The state the screens bind to, the event their buttons send, and the
// handful of systems that move both. The screens themselves are data —
// open `hud.bsn` in the editor to move, restyle or rewire them.

/// Which screen is up. The hud's bindings read this resource: each
/// screen's `Visibility` is bound to one of the bools, so flipping a
/// bool here is the whole show/hide mechanism. A run starts on the
/// menu.
#[derive(Resource, Reflect)]
#[reflect(Resource, Default)]
pub struct GameState {
    pub menu: bool,
    pub paused: bool,
    pub game_over: bool,
}

impl Default for GameState {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            menu: true,
            paused: false,
            game_over: false,
        }
    }
}

/// What a hud button says. The button's `Action` binding in `hud.bsn`
/// sends this with `button` filled from its literal (`"start"`,
/// `"resume"`, `"restart"`) — one event for every screen's buttons:
/// the payload, not the type, says what was pressed.
#[derive(Event, Reflect, Clone, Default)]
#[reflect(Event, Default)]
pub struct ScreenPressed {
    pub button: String,
}

/// The health the hud reads, live on the player. The component is the
/// run's state; `GameConfig.player_health` is the author's starting
/// number for it — `apply_health_from_config` below hands one to the
/// other. Same split as the falling cube's `speed` (component, live)
/// against the config's `move_speed` (asset, author intent).
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct PlayerHealth {
    pub current: f32,
    pub max: f32,
}

/// How much one hit takes off, wherever it comes from (the hazard
/// sensors with the physics module, the Interact key without).
const PLAYER_DAMAGE: f32 = 15.0;

/// Where a run begins — spawn and restart both use it, so a restart
/// lands exactly where the game started.
const PLAYER_SPAWN: Vec3 = Vec3::new(-2.0, 1.0, 0.0);

/// current/max -> 0..1 for the health bar's fill. Registered in
/// `GamePlugin` above: a binding's `via` can only call functions the
/// game has registered, and this one turns two component fields into
/// the one number a bar wants.
fn ratio(current: f32, max: f32) -> f32 {
    (current / max).clamp(0.0, 1.0)
}

/// Boolean not, for bindings that show a thing while a flag is false
/// (the hud hides itself while the menu is up). Registered like
/// `ratio`.
fn not(value: bool) -> bool {
    !value
}

/// Zero means the run is over — registered like `ratio` (the
/// `is_zero`-bound veil in `hud_scenario` is the pattern; this game
/// drives its game-over screen off `GameState` instead and keeps the
/// function here as the other half of the pair your own bindings
/// reach for).
fn is_zero(value: f32) -> bool {
    value == 0.0
}

/// The game UI, spawned after the main scene: the document carries a
/// `HudRoot` marker (authored on its root entity in `hud.bsn`), and
/// `wire_hud` finds it by that marker.
fn spawn_hud(mut commands: Commands, asset_server: Res<AssetServer>) {
    commands.spawn(JackdawSceneRoot(asset_server.load("hud.bsn")));
}

/// The marker the hud document itself carries, so the game can tell
/// the UI root from everything else it spawns. The `Default` here is
/// load-bearing: the document's bare `HudRoot` line applies through
/// `ReflectDefault`.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default)]
pub struct HudRoot;

/// Point the hud at the player: once the document spawns, its root
/// gets a `BindContext(player)` and every binding inside resolves the
/// player's components (the health below, the pickup counter the
/// physics module adds) against that entity. This insert is the whole
/// wiring — nothing else needs to know the UI exists.
fn wire_hud(
    mut commands: Commands,
    hud_roots: Query<Entity, Added<HudRoot>>,
    players: Query<Entity, With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    let (Ok(root), Ok(player)) = (hud_roots.single(), players.single()) else {
        return;
    };
    commands.entity(root).insert(BindContext(player));
}

/// What the hud's buttons do. The bindings send `ScreenPressed`; this
/// observer is the whole behavior — Start leaves the menu, Resume
/// unpauses, Restart puts the run back the way it began (full health,
/// spawn point, screens cleared; the physics module adds its own
/// restart half for pickups and velocity).
fn on_screen_pressed(
    press: On<ScreenPressed>,
    mut state: ResMut<GameState>,
    mut players: Query<(&mut PlayerHealth, &mut Transform), With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    match press.event().button.as_str() {
        "start" => state.menu = false,
        "resume" => state.paused = false,
        "restart" => {
            state.menu = false;
            state.paused = false;
            state.game_over = false;
            for (mut health, mut transform) in &mut players {
                health.current = health.max;
                *transform = Transform::from_translation(PLAYER_SPAWN);
            }
        }
        _ => {}
    }
}

/// The Pause action stops being a log line: Escape (or Start) flips
/// `paused` and the pause screen's visibility follows. The Resume
/// button lands on the same bool through `on_screen_pressed` — one
/// piece of state, two doors in.
fn handle_pause_action(actions: Res<GameActions>, mut state: ResMut<GameState>) {
    if actions.just_pressed("Pause") && !state.menu && !state.game_over {
        state.paused = !state.paused;
    }
}

/// The config hands its `player_health` to the component the run
/// actually reads: the config is the author's starting number, the
/// component is live state (damage moves it; the config does not
/// follow back). Runs on config change, so the file's first load and
/// an editor mid-run edit both carry — the same shape as gravity.
fn apply_health_from_config(
    config: Res<GameConfig>,
    mut players: Query<&mut PlayerHealth, With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    if !config.is_changed() {
        return;
    }
    for mut health in &mut players {
        health.max = config.player_health;
        health.current = config.player_health;
    }
}

/// Damage lands here (the hazard sensors below, or the Interact key
/// when a game has no physics module). Clamped at zero; the game-over
/// check is what reacts.
fn take_damage(health: &mut PlayerHealth, amount: f32) {
    health.current = (health.current - amount).max(0.0);
}

/// Blood out means game over: the screen's visibility follows
/// `GameState.game_over`, `move_player` stands down, and Restart —
/// button or key — brings the run back.
fn update_game_over(
    mut state: ResMut<GameState>,
    mut players: Query<&mut PlayerHealth, With<PlayerMarker>>,
) {
    if state.game_over {
        return;
    }
    for health in &mut players {
        if health.current <= 0.0 {
            state.game_over = true;
            state.paused = false;
        }
    }
}
